# Credentials, and nothing else.
#
# Everything that is not a secret moved to ~/.claude-on-the-fly/config.yaml, which is
# seeded from a commented template the first time a daemon starts. Open that file
# rather than working out a schema from here: it has room to say *why* a host is
# allowed or a CLI is brokered, which for a list where every entry is an accepted
# covert channel is the half that matters.
#
# The old environment variables still work, and still win over the file, so nothing
# breaks by leaving them here. They are undocumented now and will not gain new
# options; the daemon logs once, per variable, naming the config key that replaced it.
#
# Why the split: all four entrypoints call `load_dotenv()` with no argument, so
# python-dotenv searches upward from the *cwd*. This file is read when the TUI
# supervisor injects it into a daemon child, or when you launch from
# ~/.claude-on-the-fly, and not otherwise. config.yaml is resolved from an absolute
# path and reads the same however the daemon started. Secrets stay here because this
# is where a secret scanner looks for them.

# Telegram (required for claude-telegram)
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=

# Slack (required for claude-slack)
SLACK_APP_TOKEN=xapp-...
# Bearer token, kind inferred from the prefix:
#   xoxp-... replies as you, sees everything you can see.
#   xoxb-... replies as the app; the bot must be in each channel (fine for DMs).
# Approve/deny buttons only reach a bot-token install, so tool approvals need xoxb-.
SLACK_TOKEN=xoxb-...

# Optional worker-specific reply token. Prefer a bot token; inheriting a user
# SLACK_TOKEN can make Slack ingest a job result as a new request.
# JOBS_SLACK_TOKEN=xoxb-...

# With sandbox.mode off, model API keys may be inherited directly. Under env or
# jail, provision supported keys in the host keychain instead; see
# docs/how-to/enable-sandboxing.md.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Log verbosity: DEBUG | INFO | WARNING | ERROR. Stays an environment variable
# because logging is configured before config.yaml can be parsed, and a complaint
# about that file needs somewhere to go.
#
# Message text in the log follows this: redacted to a length at INFO and above,
# present at DEBUG. There is no separate switch, because DEBUG already dumps whole
# Slack events and slack_bolt payloads either way. Treat a DEBUG log as unshareable:
# ~/.claude-on-the-fly is shaped for a file syncer, so anything in it should be
# assumed to leave the machine and to sit there for logs.keep_days.
# LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# In config.yaml now:
#
#   sandbox:      whether the agent ever holds a real credential (was COTF_SANDBOX*)
#   agent:        backend, mode, model (was AGENT_BACKEND / *_MODE / *_MODEL)
#   egress:       hosts reachable without an approval prompt
#   commands:     credentialed CLIs reachable through the command broker
#   permissions:  whether tool calls are gated, and how much you get asked
#   slack:        allowed/blocked/silent senders, footer, slash command, limits
#   telegram:     allowed user, footer
#   jobs:         queue adapter, concurrency, polling, timeout
#   logs:         retention, host tag
#
# Saving that file is enough; it is re-read when it changes. The few settings read
# once at startup (a bound socket, a PATH shim, whether a service exists at all) tell
# you on the next turn that they need a restart, rather than quietly running the old
# value.
#
# Approval prompts go to the conversation whose agent triggered them, so there is no
# routing to configure. Work with no conversation behind it (cron, the job queue) has
# nobody to ask and stays ungated.
